Harden the target against inputs that would oops (and so reboot a router built
with CONFIG_PANIC_ON_OOPS=y). Every case below logs a ratelimited kernel message
and continues or rejects the rule instead of crashing:

- fullconenat_tg()/fullconenat_tg6(): nf_ct_get() can return NULL (notrack,
  conntrack table full); return XT_CONTINUE instead of dereferencing it.
- Masquerade path: only set masq_index when the out device is known.
- get_device_ip(): read ip_ptr and ifa_list through the RCU accessors.
- find_appropriate_port(): port math in u32 (no u16 wrap to 0), at most 128
  probes from a random start under the global spinlock, and never free a live
  mapping; if no port is free, do plain SNAT with the rule's range and record
  no mapping.
- fullconenat_tg_check(): reject non-IPv4 rules (fullconenat_tg6() has the same
  unbounded search and is not shipped), rules outside init_net (the mapping
  table is global), rangesize != 1, an inverted --to-source range and a
  --to-ports range starting at 0 or inverted; return nf_ct_netns_get() errors
  instead of ignoring them (destroy would then drop a reference never taken).

